itute for the same requirement; otherwise the dossier should wait for the authority to decide. If the service supports escalation or retry, that should be treated as a tracked follow-up step rather than automatic closure. The system should preserve the status, the reason for interruption, and the history of what was sent or requested so someone can pick it up again later. Background checks should continue when the provisional status is only due to a temporary dependency, such as waiting for an external register response or a delayed validation service; they should be stopped or revisited later if the source evidence changes, the user withdraws the dossier, or the underlying data has been superseded and the result would no longer be trustworthy. The dossier should remain provisional whenever checks are not yet complete and the outcome could still affect eligibility or submission readiness. For the first delivery, the must-haves are the ability to identify required evidence, collect and validate attestations, compile them into a dossier, submit or receive that package between supplier and authority, provide basic status tracking, maintain version history, and offer enough translation or viewing support for authorities to inspect what they receive. When a dossier package is updated after validation or submission, the new revision should supersede the previous one for operational use, but the older version must remain stored as an official historical record. The system should clearly mark which revision is active and which one was valid at the time of submission, so both supplier and authority can see the timeline. Only users with the proper role should be able to view the version history, and there should be no ambiguity about the current record.",
